Setting: Λ = Z/4Z and M is a free Λ-module of rank 2, so M ≅ (Z/4)^2 and Gl(M) ≅ GL_2(Z/4), of order 96. This is level-4 structure on elliptic curves, used for modular curves in characteristic ≠ 2. Let M_0 = M/2M ≅ F_2^2 and J = M_0^* = M_0 \ {0}, a set of 3 elements identified with P(M)(F_2).

(29) M^* is the set of x in M whose reduction x^0 is nonzero, i.e. the unimodular (primitive) vectors. It is the inverse image of J under M → M_0 and has 16 − 4 = 12 elements. Each fibre over j ∈ J is a coset of 2M ≅ M_0. So M^* → J is a torsor under the constant group scheme (M_0)_J over the finite set J.

(30) The unit group (Z/4)^* = {±1} acts freely on M^*, compatibly with the projection. The quotient is Δ_M = M^*/{±1} = P(M)(Z/4Z), the Z/4-points of the projective line attached to M. It has 6 elements, matching |P^1(Z/p^2)| = p(p+1) = 6 for p = 2.

(31) The canonical map Δ_M → J is reduction mod 2, P(M)(Z/4) → P(M)(F_2). It is 2-to-1. Grothendieck uses this to put an intrinsic octahedron structure on Δ:
- the 6 points are the vertices;
- the 3 fibres of (31) are the 3 diagonals (pairs of opposite vertices);
- the edges are the 2-element subsets that are not fibres: C(6,2) − 3 = 12;
- the faces are the 3-element subsets mapping bijectively onto J, i.e. the global sections of (31): 2^3 = 8.

More generally, faces, edges and vertices are the sections of Δ → J over subsets of J of size 3, 2 and 1. Incidence is extension of sections, i.e. inclusion of subsets.

Purpose (continued on the next page): Gl(M) acts on Δ by octahedron automorphisms with kernel {±1}. This gives an isomorphism PGL_2(Z/4) = GL_2(Z/4)/{±1} ≅ Aut_oct(Δ) ≅ S_4 × Z/2, with the antipodal map as centre. This ties the group-theoretic diagram (28) on the previous page to the classical geometry of the octahedral level-4 modular curve. The 6 points of Δ are exactly the cusps of X(4), i.e. ±-classes of primitive vectors mod 4.
